// Repro — an inherent `Option(T)` method called on an `Option(Dyn(Trait))` is
// CALLED in the emitted C but never DEFINED or DECLARED, so the C fails to
// compile. Nothing in the Yo source is wrong; `yo check` passes.
//
//   yo compile issues/repros/option-of-a-trait-object-loses-its-inherent-methods.yo --optimize 2 -o /tmp/x
//
// Expected: it compiles and prints `ok`.
// Actual:   error: call to undeclared function
//           'yo_id_<n>_rtparam0_enum_r8058c2_n121_value_1500_ret_bool';
//           ISO C99 and later do not support implicit function declarations
//
// The symbol appears EXACTLY ONCE in the emitted .c — at the call site. The
// specialization is reached by the evaluator (so the call is emitted) and then
// skipped by codegen (so no body and no prototype are).
//
// `Option(i32).is_none()` is fine, so it is the trait-object payload that does
// it, not `is_none`. A `match` over the same Option is the shape that works.
//
// See issues/option-of-a-trait-object-never-emits-its-inherent-methods.md
{ assert } :: import("std/assert");
{ AnyError } :: import("std/error");
{ println } :: import("std/fmt");
main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  (o : Option(AnyError)) = Option(AnyError).None;
  assert(o.is_none(), "a None is none");
  println("ok");
});
export(main);
